berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/equivocates: A1: "unconceived" is taken as conceiving the objects while they are not being conceived at all, even by the one now conceiving them in A1 and as conceiving, as the content of one's thought, that the objects exist with no one thinking of them in the imagined scene where conceive_unconceived is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C1 (S5) does not follow (berkeley-master.logic.r2.sense1)
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/countermodel: C1 (S5): conceive_mind_indep = T, conceive_unconceived = T, conceive_unconceived_2 = F, repugnant = T
